"## History of quantum computing: Feynman's 1981/1982 proposal, David Deutsch's universal quantum computer, key milestones timeline — per Wikipedia articles 'Quantum computing' and 'Quantum information science'\n# History of Quantum Computing — Findings\n\n**Sources:** [1] Wikipedia, *Quantum computing*; [2] Wikipedia, *Quantum information science*\n\n## Prehistory and foundations\n\n- 1930s–1960s: Quantum mechanics matured; the EPR paradox (1935) and Bell's theorem (1964) established the nonlocality/entanglement questions central to quantum information [1][2].\n- 1960–1961: Rolf Landauer's principle showed that irreversible logical operations dissipate a minimum energy (kT ln 2 per erased bit) [1].\n- 1973: Charles Bennett showed that computation can in principle be performed reversibly, linking information theory to thermodynamics [1][2].\n- ~1970: Stephen Wiesner invented \"conjugate coding\" (quantum money, precursor to quantum cryptography), though it was only published in 1983 [2].\n- 1973: Alexander Holevo proved the Holevo bound, limiting how much classical information a quantum channel can carry [2].\n- 1976: The term \"quantum computer\" (Polish *komputer kwantowy*) appeared in a Polish technical dictionary [1].\n\n## Feynman's proposal (1981/1982)\n\n- 1980: Paul Benioff first described a quantum-mechanical model of the Turing machine, showing quantum systems could be computationally universal [1].\n- 1980: Yuri Manin independently suggested in *Computable and Uncomputable* that quantum systems might require quantum machines to simulate [1].\n- May 1981: At the First Conference on the Physics of Computation (MIT), Richard Feynman delivered his keynote \"Simulating Physics with Computers,\" arguing classical computers cannot efficiently simulate quantum systems; he proposed computers built on quantum mechanics for this task, famously stating \"Nature isn't classical… and if you want to make a simulation of nature, you'd better make it quantum mechanical\" [1].\n- 1982: The talk was published in the *International Journal of Theoretical Physics* [1].\n\n## David Deutsch's universal quantum computer\n\n- 1985: David Deutsch published \"Quantum theory, the Church–Turing principle and the universal quantum computer\" (*Proceedings of the Royal Society A*), describing the first **universal quantum computer** — a quantum analogue of the universal Turing machine — and articulating the Church–Turing principle in quantum terms [1].\n- 1985: Deutsch also devised the first quantum algorithm (Deutsch's algorithm) demonstrating a quantum speedup; it was generalized to the Deutsch–Jozsa algorithm in 1992 [1][2].\n\n## Key milestones timeline [1][2]\n\n| Year | Milestone |\n|---|---|\n| 1984 | Bennett & Brassard: BB84 quantum key distribution protocol [2] |\n| 1992 | Deutsch–Jozsa algorithm [1] |\n| 1993 | Bennett et al.: quantum teleportation protocol; Bernstein–Vazirani algorithm; Simon's algorithm (1994) shows exponential oracle speedup [1][2] |\n| 1994 | Peter Shor: polynomial-time quantum factoring/discrete-log algorithm (Shor's algorithm), sparking mainstream interest [1] |\n| 1995 | Shor's 9-qubit quantum error-correcting code; Steane code (1996); first quantum logic gate demonstrated (NIST, trapped Be⁺ ion, Monroe & Wineland) [1] |\n| 1996 | Grover's search algorithm (quadratic speedup); Seth Lloyd shows quantum simulation efficiency [1] |\n| 1997 | Quantum teleportation demonstrated experimentally (Zeilinger group, Innsbruck) [2] |\n| 1998 | First working 2-qubit NMR quantum computer (Chuang, Gershenfeld, Kubinec), executing Deutsch's algorithm [1] |\n| 2000 | DiVincenzo criteria for physical quantum computers published [1] |\n| 2001 | Shor's algorithm run on a 7-qubit NMR machine at IBM, factoring 15 = 3 × 5 [1] |\n| 2007 | D-Wave demos a 16-qubit adiabatic processor [1] |\n| 2011 | D-Wave One (128 qubits) — first commercially sold quantum computer (to Lockheed Martin) [1] |\n| 2012 | Nobel Prize in Physics to Haroche & Wineland for quantum-state manipulation experiments [1] |\n| 2016 | IBM Quantum Experience: cloud access to a 5-qubit superconducting processor [1] |\n| 2019 | Google's 53-qubit Sycamore claims \"quantum supremacy\" via random-circuit sampling (IBM disputed the classical-comparison estimate) [1] |\n| 2020 | China's photonic Jiuzhang claims quantum advantage via boson sampling [1] |\n| 2021–2023 | IBM Eagle (127 qubits, 2021), Osprey (433, 2022), Condor (1,121, 2023) [1] |\n| 2024 | Google's Willow chip demonstrates quantum error correction improving as system size scales (\"below threshold\") [1] |\n| 2025 | Microsoft announces the Majorana 1 topological-qubit chip (claim under scientific scrutiny) [1] |\n\n## Related quantum information science milestones [2]\n\n- 1982: No-cloning theorem formalized (Wootters & Zurek; Dieks).\n- 1991: Ekert's E91 entanglement-based QKD protocol.\n- 1992: Superdense coding (Bennett & Wiesner).\n- 1995–1996: Quantum error correction established as a practical framework (Shor, Steane).\n- 2016–2017: China's Micius satellite achieves satellite-based QKD and intercontinental quantum-secured communication.\n\n**Summary:** The field traces to reversible-computing theory (Landauer, Bennett), crystallized in Feynman's 1981/1982 argument that quantum physics demands quantum simulation hardware, and was formalized by Deutsch's 1985 universal quantum computer. Shor's 1994 factoring algorithm transformed it into a major research priority, followed by error correction (1995), experimental NMR demonstrations (1998–2001), commercial hardware (2011), and quantum-advantage claims (2019–2020) [1][2].\n\n---\n\n## Physical qubit implementations: superconducting circuits, trapped ions, photonics, neutral atoms, spin qubits, topological qubits — per Wikipedia articles 'Qubit' and 'Quantum computing' hardware sections\n# Physical Qubit Implementations — Summary\n\nBased on the Wikipedia articles **Qubit** (§ \"Physical implementations\") [1] and **Quantum computing** (hardware/physical realization sections) [2]. Note: Wikipedia is continuously edited; figures below reflect content as of my knowledge cutoff.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Superconducting circuits [1][2]\n- **Substrate/encoding:** Nonlinear LC circuits built from Josephson junctions (transmon, flux, phase, fluxonium variants); qubit states are macroscopic charge/flux/phase quantum states, read out via microwave resonators.\n- **Operation:** Dilution refrigerators at ~10–20 mK; microwave-pulse gates.\n- **Strengths:** Fast gates (~10–100 ns), lithographic fabrication, mature control electronics.\n- **Weaknesses:** Short coherence (typically tens to hundreds of µs), millimeter-scale devices limiting density, cryogenic/wiring overhead.\n- **Notable:** Google Sycamore 53-qubit \"quantum supremacy\" claim (2019); IBM Eagle (127), Osprey (433), Condor (1,121) qubits. Vendors: IBM, Google, Rigetti, IQM.\n\n## 2. Trapped ions [1][2]\n- **Encoding:** Hyperfine (e.g., ¹⁷¹Yb⁺) or optical electronic states (e.g., ⁴⁰Ca⁺) of ions confined in RF Paul traps.\n- **Gates:** Laser-driven single-qubit rotations; two-qubit entanglement via shared motional modes (Cirac–Zoller, Mølmer–Sørensen).\n- **Strengths:** Very long coherence (minutes or more), highest reported two-qubit fidelities (>99.9%), identical particles, all-to-all connectivity within a chain.\n- **Weaknesses:** Slow gates (µs–ms); scaling needs ion shuttling or modular trap architectures; complex laser systems.\n- **Notable:** IonQ, Quantinuum (H1/H2 series, 56-qubit H2 with high fidelities), Alpine Quantum Technologies.\n\n## 3. Photonics [1][2]\n- **Encoding:** Single-photon degrees of freedom — polarization, path, time-bin, or orbital angular momentum.\n- **Strengths:** Room-temperature operation, minimal decoherence in flight, natural fit for quantum communication/telecom.\n- **Weaknesses:** Photons barely interact — two-qubit gates are probabilistic; leading approaches use measurement-based (cluster-state) computing; deterministic single-photon sources and efficient detectors remain hard.\n- **Notable:** Jiuzhang boson sampler (USTC); Xanadu (Borealis), PsiQuantum, Quandela.\n\n## 4. Neutral atoms [1][2]\n- **Encoding:** Hyperfine or nuclear-spin states of alkali/alkaline-earth atoms (Rb, Cs, Sr) held in optical tweezers or optical lattices.\n- **Gates:** Microwave/Raman single-qubit pulses; two-qubit entanglement via Rydberg blockade.\n- **Strengths:** Identical atoms, reconfigurable 2D/3D arrays scaling to hundreds–thousands of atoms, long coherence.\n- **Weaknesses:** Atom loss; gate fidelities historically below ions/superconducting (improving rapidly); µs-scale gates.\n- **Notable:** QuEra Aquila (256 qubits), Pasqal, Atom Computing (~1,000+-atom array), academic arrays (Harvard/Lukin, Caltech).\n\n## 5. Spin qubits (semiconductor quantum dots & defects) [1][2]\n- **Encoding:** Electron or hole spin in Si/GaAs quantum dots; donor spins (P in ²⁸Si); defect centers (NV centers in diamond).\n- **Strengths:** Nanoscale footprint, CMOS-compatible fabrication (especially silicon), long spin coherence in isotopically purified ²⁸Si; NV centers work at room temperature (mostly used for sensing and small demos).\n- **Weaknesses:** Charge noise, valley effects; two-qubit fidelities (~99–99.5%) still below ion/superconducting leaders.\n- **Notable:** Intel silicon spin chips, Silicon Quantum Computing, Diraq.\n\n## 6. Topological qubits [1][2]\n- **Concept:** Nonlocal encoding in Majorana zero modes / non-Abelian anyons for intrinsic (hardware-level) protection against local noise, potentially slashing error-correction overhead.\n- **Status:** Largely proposed/experimental; unambiguous demonstration of braiding-based qubit operation remains contested. Microsoft pursues Majorana devices (semiconductor–superconductor hybrids); Wikipedia treats this as the least mature platform.\n\n---\n\n## Cross-cutting points [2]\n- Platforms are evaluated against **DiVincenzo criteria**: scalable, well-characterized qubits; reliable initialization; coherence long vs. gate time; universal gate set; high-fidelity measurement.\n- All platforms have error rates requiring **quantum error correction**, with large overheads (hundreds–thousands of physical qubits per logical qubit).\n- Scaling bottlenecks differ: control wiring/cryogenics (superconducting), laser delivery and trap control (ions, atoms), photon loss (photonics), fabrication variability (spins).\n- Wikipedia emphasizes the field has **not converged** on a single platform; modular and hybrid architectures are active research directions.\n\n**Sources:** [1] Wikipedia, \"Qubit\" — Physical implementations section; [2] Wikipedia, \"Quantum computing\" — hardware/physical realization sections.\n\n---\n\n## Major quantum algorithms and their speedups: Shor's algorithm, Grover's algorithm, quantum Fourier transform, variational quantum algorithms — per Wikipedia articles 'Quantum algorithm', 'Shor's algorithm', 'Grover's algorithm'\n# Major Quantum Algorithms and Their Speedups\n\n**Sources referenced:**\n[1] Wikipedia: *Quantum algorithm* · [2] Wikipedia: *Shor's algorithm* · [3] Wikipedia: *Grover's algorithm* · [4] Wikipedia: *Quantum Fourier transform* · [5] Wikipedia: *Variational quantum algorithm*\n\n---\n\n## Overview [1]\nQuantum algorithms run on models of quantum computation (typically the quantum circuit model) and fall into major classes: Fourier-transform-based algorithms (Shor's, phase estimation), quantum search/amplitude amplification (Grover), quantum simulation, and hybrid quantum–classical (variational) methods. Quantum computers do **not** speed up all problems — many tasks show no advantage — but certain structured problems (factoring, discrete logarithms, quantum simulation) admit exponential speedups, while unstructured search admits a provably optimal quadratic one. The class of problems efficiently solvable by quantum computers is **BQP**. [1]\n\n## Shor's Algorithm [2]\n- **Purpose:** Integer factorization (and discrete logarithms) in polynomial time.\n- **Origin:** Peter Shor, 1994.\n- **Speedup:** **Exponential** over the best known classical algorithms (general number field sieve is sub-exponential). Gate complexity is roughly O((log N)³), improvable to ~O((log N)²·log log N·log log log N) with fast multiplication.\n- **Method:** Reduces factoring to **order-finding**, solved via the quantum Fourier transform on ~2 log N qubits, followed by classical continued-fraction post-processing; success probability is a constant that can be amplified by repetition.\n- **Impact:** Breaks RSA and related cryptosystems; experimentally demonstrated on small numbers (e.g., factoring 15 in 2001 via NMR). [2]\n\n## Grover's Algorithm [3]\n- **Purpose:** Searching an unstructured database (black-box function) of N items for a marked entry.\n- **Origin:** Lov Grover, 1996.\n- **Speedup:** **Quadratic** — O(√N) oracle queries vs. O(N) classically, with ~π/4·√(N/M) iterations for M solutions.\n- **Optimality:** Provably optimal; no quantum algorithm can beat Ω(√N) queries (Bennett–Bernstein–Brassard–Vazirani bound).\n- **Method:** Amplitude amplification — repeated oracle calls plus the diffusion operator (inversion about the mean) to amplify the marked state's amplitude.\n- **Applications:** Exhaustive search over NP-problem solution spaces (only quadratic gain), collision problems, and halving effective symmetric-key strength (e.g., AES-128 → ~2⁶⁴ quantum operations). Generalized as **amplitude amplification** and **quantum counting**. [3]\n\n## Quantum Fourier Transform (QFT) [1][4]\n- Maps a basis state |x⟩ to the normalized superposition Σₖ e^(2πikx/N)|k⟩ over N = 2ⁿ basis states.\n- **Speedup:** Implemented with only **O(n²) quantum gates**, versus O(N log N) = O(n·2ⁿ) for the classical FFT — an *exponential* gate-count reduction.\n- **Caveat:** The speedup is not directly exploitable, since the output amplitudes cannot be read out wholesale; QFT's power comes as a **subroutine** in Shor's algorithm and quantum phase estimation. [1][4]\n\n## Variational Quantum Algorithms (VQAs) [1][5]\n- **Type:** Hybrid quantum–classical schemes; a parameterized quantum circuit (ansatz) is evaluated on a quantum processor while a classical optimizer tunes the parameters.\n- **Key examples:** **VQE** (Variational Quantum Eigensolver, 2014) for molecular ground-state energies; **QAOA** (Quantum Approximate Optimization Algorithm, 2014) for combinatorial optimization.\n- **Motivation:** Suited to **NISQ** (noisy intermediate-scale quantum) devices, requiring shallow circuits.\n- **Speedup status:** **No proven speedup** — performance is heuristic; known challenges include barren plateaus (vanishing gradients), trainability/local-minima issues, and classical simulability of some ansatzes. [1][5]\n\n## Speedup Comparison\n\n| Algorithm | Problem | Speedup class | Proven optimal? |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Shor's [2] | Factoring, discrete log | Exponential | vs. best known classical (no superpolynomial classical proof) |\n| Grover's [3] | Unstructured search | Quadratic (O(√N)) | Yes — Ω(√N) lower bound |\n| QFT [4] | Fourier transform | Exponential in gate count | Not directly measurable; subroutine only |\n| VQAs [5] | Chemistry, optimization | None proven | Heuristic / NISQ-oriented |\n\n**Key takeaway:** Shor's algorithm is the flagship exponential speedup; Grover's offers the best possible quadratic gain for unstructured search; QFT delivers exponential gate savings but mainly as an internal component; VQAs trade guaranteed speedup for near-term hardware feasibility. [1]–[5]\n\n---\n\n## Quantum error correction, decoherence, and fault tolerance: surface codes, logical qubits, threshold theorem — per Wikipedia articles 'Quantum error correction', 'Quantum decoherence', 'Threshold theorem'\n# Quantum Error Correction, Decoherence & Fault Tolerance — Summary\n\n**Sources:** [1] Wikipedia: *Quantum error correction* · [2] Wikipedia: *Quantum decoherence* · [3] Wikipedia: *Threshold theorem*\n\n---\n\n## 1. Quantum Error Correction (QEC) [1]\n\n**Why it's hard (vs. classical codes):**\n- The no-cloning theorem forbids simply copying quantum states for backup.\n- Errors are continuous (not discrete bit flips), and direct measurement of qubits destroys superpositions.\n- QEC circumvents this by encoding a **logical qubit** non-locally across many entangled **physical qubits**, then measuring only *error syndromes* (parity-type checks) that reveal the error without revealing (or collapsing) the encoded data.\n\n**Core formalism:**\n- Any single-qubit error decomposes into a discrete set: bit-flip (**X**), phase-flip (**Z**), and combined (**Y = iXZ**) — so correcting these corrects arbitrary errors.\n- **Shor's 9-qubit code** (1995) was the first to correct an arbitrary error on a single qubit; **Steane's 7-qubit code** and **CSS codes** followed; **stabilizer codes** (Gottesman) provide the general framework. The smallest code correcting an arbitrary single-qubit error uses **5 physical qubits**.\n- Correction validity is characterized by the **Knill–Laflamme conditions**.\n\n**Surface codes:**\n- A topological family (descended from Kitaev's toric code) with qubits on a 2-D lattice and stabilizer measurements on adjacent plaquettes/vertices, requiring only **nearest-neighbor interactions** — a major practical advantage.\n- They tolerate a relatively high physical error rate: threshold ≈ **1%** (often quoted ~0.75–1% for circuit-level noise), the highest among widely studied codes.\n- A distance-*d* surface code corrects up to ⌊(d−1)/2⌋ errors; logical qubits correspond to non-contractible loop operators; unrotated codes need ~2d² qubits, rotated variants ~d².\n\n**Fault tolerance & logical qubits:**\n- Fault-tolerant design ensures errors don't cascade (e.g., transversal gates, careful syndrome-extraction circuits). The **Eastin–Knill theorem** forbids a universal gate set from transversal gates alone, so schemes like **magic state distillation** supply non-Clifford gates (e.g., T gates).\n- Experiments: logical-qubit demonstrations in trapped ions, superconducting circuits, and neutral atoms; Google's Willow chip (2024) demonstrated below-threshold operation, where enlarging the code *reduces* logical error rates exponentially.\n\n## 2. Quantum Decoherence [2]\n\n- **Definition:** the loss of quantum coherence when a system becomes entangled with its environment; off-diagonal terms (coherences) of the system's reduced density matrix decay, making quantum interference unobservable and the system behave classically.\n- Decoherence is fully **unitary** for the combined system+environment; the apparent \"collapse\" is only for the subsystem — it explains the quantum-to-classical transition but (per the article) does not by itself solve the measurement problem.\n- Key concepts: **einselection** and **pointer states** (Zurek) — the environment stably \"monitors\" certain preferred states; **quantum Darwinism** describes how classical information about them proliferates.\n- Characterization in devices: **T₁** (energy relaxation) and **T₂** (dephasing) times, with T₂ ≤ 2T₁. Decoherence has been measured in interferometry with large molecules (e.g., fullerenes) and underlies environmental noise models.\n- Relevance to computing: decoherence is the dominant obstacle to maintaining superpositions; QEC and fault-tolerant design exist precisely to keep logical information coherent longer than the physical qubits' coherence times [1][2].\n\n## 3. Threshold Theorem [3]\n\n- **Statement:** if the error rate per physical component (gate, qubit, measurement) is below a constant **threshold** p_th, then arbitrarily long quantum computations can be performed with only **polylogarithmic overhead** in the number of physical qubits/time, via concatenated or topological error-correcting codes.\n- Proved in the mid-1990s–early 2000s by **Aharonov & Ben-Or**, **Kitaev**, **Knill–Laflamme–Zurek**, building on **Shor's** (1996) fault-tolerant constructions.\n- **Typical threshold values:** early concatenated-code estimates were pessimistic (~10⁻⁶–10⁻⁴); modern **surface codes allow ~1%** error rates, the most forgiving of standard schemes.\n- **Assumptions:** noise is local/weakly correlated, operations can be parallelized, fresh (ancilla) qubits are available, and measurements/reset are possible. Below threshold, logical error rates are suppressed exponentially with code distance; above threshold, adding qubits makes things *worse*.\n- **Significance:** the theorem is the foundation for scalable quantum computing — it shows physical noise rates need only be reduced below a constant (~1% for surface codes), not to zero, for arbitrarily reliable computation [1][3].\n\n---\n\n### Key numbers at a glance\n| Quantity | Value | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Smallest arbitrary-error-correcting code | 5 qubits | [1] |\n| Surface-code threshold | ~0.75–1% | [1][3] |\n| Early concatenated thresholds | ~10⁻⁶–10⁻⁴ | [3] |\n| Overhead below threshold | polylogarithmic (distance-d surface code: ~d² qubits) | [1][3] |\n| Coherence times | T₂ ≤ 2T₁ | [2] |\n\n---\n\n## Quantum supremacy/advantage claims and industry landscape: Google Sycamore 2019, IBM Quantum, D-Wave, national quantum initiatives — per Wikipedia articles 'Quantum supremacy', 'Sycamore processor', 'IBM Quantum'\n# Quantum Supremacy/Advantage Claims & Industry Landscape\n\n## Terminology and definition\n- \"Quantum supremacy\" was coined by Caltech physicist John Preskill in 2012, meaning a quantum device solving a problem infeasible for any classical computer. Many researchers (notably IBM) prefer **\"quantum advantage\"**, which implies practical usefulness; random-circuit sampling (RCS) and boson sampling are the standard demonstration tasks [1].\n\n## Google Sycamore (2019) — the landmark claim\n- On 23 October 2019, Google AI published in *Nature* (\"Quantum supremacy using a programmable superconducting processor\") that its **53-qubit Sycamore** chip (one of 54 transmon qubits was nonfunctional, arranged in a square lattice with tunable couplers) sampled random quantum circuits in **~200 seconds**, claiming the equivalent task would take **10,000 years** on Summit, then the world's fastest supercomputer [1][2].\n- **IBM immediately disputed the claim** (Pednault et al., 21 Oct 2019), arguing with better classical methods Summit could do it in **~2.5 days** — still a 1,000× quantum speedup, but not \"supremacy\" [1][3].\n- Later classical advances eroded the gap: a 2022 tensor-network method (Pan, Zhang & Zhang) simulated the Sycamore circuits in ~15 hours on 512 GPUs, with subsequent work cutting this further, so the original 10,000-year estimate is considered superseded [1][2].\n- Sycamore was subsequently used for Hartree–Fock chemistry simulation (2020), a discrete time-crystal experiment (2022), and surface-code error-correction scaling (2023). Google's successor chip **Willow** (105 qubits, Dec 2024) demonstrated below-threshold quantum error correction and a much larger RCS claim (~10²⁵ years classical, also contested) [2].\n\n## Other advantage claims\n- **China/USTC (Pan Jianwei group):** photonic **Jiuzhang** (Dec 2020) performed boson sampling in 200 s vs. a claimed 2.5 billion years classically; superconducting **Zuchongzhi 2.1** (56 qubits, 2021) also claimed advantage [1].\n- **D-Wave (Canada, founded 1999):** sells *quantum annealers*, not gate-model machines. Shipped D-Wave One (128 qubits) to Lockheed Martin in 2011 and D-Wave Two (512 qubits) to a Google/NASA lab in 2013; early studies (e.g., Troyer et al., 2014) found **no scaling speedup**, and whether its devices behave quantum-mechanically was long contested. Google ended its D-Wave partnership in 2019. D-Wave's Advantage (5,000+ qubits, 2020) and Advantage2 (2025) target optimization; in 2025 D-Wave claimed a supremacy-class result simulating magnetic materials in *Science* — quickly disputed by classical simulation groups [1][4].\n\n## IBM Quantum program\n- IBM opened the **IBM Quantum Experience** (5-qubit cloud device) in May 2016 — the first cloud-accessible quantum computer — and launched the IBM Q Network in Dec 2017 (JPMorgan, Daimler, etc.) [3].\n- Hardware roadmap: Falcon 27 (2019) → Hummingbird 65 (2020) → **Eagle 127 (Nov 2021, first >100-qubit)** → **Osprey 433 (Nov 2022)** → **Condor 1,121 qubits (Dec 2023)**; IBM then pivoted to quality-focused **Heron** (133–156 qubits) and modular **Quantum System Two** (2023), with a roadmap to fault-tolerant **Starling (~2029)** and **Blue Jay (~2033)** [3].\n- Installations include Quantum System One (Poughkeepsie 2019; Germany 2021; Japan 2021; Cleveland Clinic 2022). **Qiskit**, its open-source SDK (2017), is a de facto industry standard [3].\n- IBM consistently frames its goal as *quantum advantage* on useful problems rather than supremacy demonstrations [1][3].\n\n## National quantum initiatives\n- **US:** National Quantum Initiative Act (signed Dec 2018), ~$1.2B over five years, funding DOE labs, NSF institutes, and NIST [1].\n- **EU:** Quantum Flagship, ~€1B over 10 years (launched 2018) [1].\n- **China:** ~$10B National Laboratory for Quantum Information Sciences (Hefei); Micius quantum satellite (2016) [1].\n- **UK:** National Quantum Technologies Programme (~£1B from 2014; £2.5B ten-year strategy 2023). Similar programs exist in Japan, Canada (C$360M strategy, 2023), and India (National Quantum Mission, 2023) [1].\n\n## Bottom line\nGoogle's 2019 Sycamore result was the first widely publicized supremacy claim but was disputed by IBM within days and later weakened by improved classical algorithms; the field has since shifted toward \"quantum advantage\" demonstrations (Jiuzhang, Zuchongzhi, Willow, D-Wave 2025), all of which face classical-algorithm rebuttals, while industry consolidation around IBM's gate-model roadmap and national funding programs has grown dramatically [1][2][3][4].\n\n---\n**Sources:**\n[1] Wikipedia — \"Quantum supremacy\"\n[2] Wikipedia — \"Sycamore processor\"\n[3] Wikipedia — \"IBM Quantum\"\n[4] Wikipedia — \"D-Wave Systems\""